
  [1mThe main choice: build the page from the vendored Keel kit instead of copying [38;5;153mservices.js[39m.[0m The Services page was
  written before the kit existed and hand-builds its table and filter form. The kit's components already do what the
  spec describes:
  - [38;5;153mdataTable[39m builds the sort links: clicking the active column flips its direction, other columns start ascending, with
    the ▲/▼ arrows and the accessibility attributes.
  - [38;5;153mfilterBar[39m with [38;5;153mselectField[39m keeps the current sort when you change the filter, and submits on change.
  - [38;5;153mbadge[39m gives the green, red, amber and blue status chips.
  - [38;5;153mpageHeader[39m and [38;5;153memptyState[39m cover the header and the "No deploys in staging" message.

  So Deploys behaves like Services, but its markup and styling are the kit's rather than a copy of Services. If you'd
  rather it look exactly like Services, that's the decision to change before implementing.

  [1mTwo tasks:[0m
  1. [1m[38;5;153msrc/pages/deploys.js[39m and its tests.[0m Nine rendering tests cover the header, the default order, both sorts, the
     chips, the duration format, the filter, the empty state, and the fallback for unknown query values.
  2. [1mRoute and nav link.[0m One route entry in [38;5;153msrc/server.js[39m (which also brings the existing 503 handling), the nav link
     after Services, server tests for the page and its 503, and a one-line README update.

  [1mThree things the spec didn't settle, which I decided:[0m
  - [1mOrder within a service:[0m deploys of the same service stay newest first, whichever way you sort by Service.
  - [1mMissing [38;5;153m?dir=[39m:[0m Service defaults to ascending and Started to newest first. The page's own links always include [38;5;153mdir[39m,
    so this only affects hand-typed URLs.
  - [1mEmpty snapshot with "All environments":[0m the page says "No deploys" rather than "No deploys in all".

  [1mChecking:[0m I ran all of the plan's code in a throwaway copy of the repo. The new tests fail before the code exists, and
  the full suite passes afterwards (29 tests). I also confirmed the tie-order test fails if the newest-first step is
  removed.

  Codex isn't installed, so the plan only got my own self-review and no second review. I logged that as an unreviewed
  gate.

  Once you've read it, tell me if anything should change or if you want me to start implementing.

